.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt XFT_XID 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_xid_is_start ,
.Nm xft_xid_is_continue ,
.Nm xft_xid_load_point ,
.Nm xft_xid_decode ,
.Nm xft_xid_codepoint_len ,
.Nm g_xid_start ,
.Nm g_xid_start_len ,
.Nm g_xid_continue ,
.Nm g_xid_continue_len
.Nd Unicode XID_Start and XID_Continue lookup, codepoint tables and UTF-8 decoding
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xid.h
.Ft bool
.Fn xft_xid_is_start "t_u32 cp"
.Ft bool
.Fn xft_xid_is_continue "t_u32 cp"
.Ft t_u32a
.Fn xft_xid_load_point "const t_u8 *restrict bytes" "t_size cap"
.Ft t_u32
.Fn xft_xid_decode "t_u32 w"
.Ft t_u8
.Fn xft_xid_codepoint_len "t_u32a codepoint"
.Vt extern const t_u32 g_xid_start[][2];
.Vt extern const t_size g_xid_start_len;
.Vt extern const t_u32 g_xid_continue[][2];
.Vt extern const t_size g_xid_continue_len;
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn xft_xid_is_start
tests whether
.Fa cp
may start an identifier: it has the
.Ql XID_Start
property, or is
.Ql _
.Pq U+005F ,
which UAX #31 lets a profile add and which
.Ql XID_Start
itself leaves out.
.Fn xft_xid_is_continue
tests whether
.Fa cp
has the
.Ql XID_Continue
property, which already includes
.Ql _ .
Any start codepoint is also a continue codepoint.
.Pp
ASCII is answered inline through
.Xr xft_isalpha 3
and
.Xr xft_isalnum 3 ;
anything above U+007F takes a branchless binary search over the matching
table.
Values past U+10FFFF, surrogates and unassigned codepoints are never in
either set.
.Pp
.Va g_xid_start
holds the codepoints with the Unicode
.Ql XID_Start
property as
.Va g_xid_start_len
inclusive ranges, and
.Va g_xid_continue
holds the codepoints with
.Ql XID_Continue
as
.Va g_xid_continue_len
inclusive ranges.
Each entry is
.Ql {lo, hi} ;
a single codepoint is stored as
.Ql {cp, cp} .
In each table the ranges are ascending and do not overlap.
Adjacent ranges are kept as they appear in the source data and are not
merged.
.Pp
Both tables live in
.Pa src/xid/xft_xidtable.c ,
which is generated; do not edit it by hand.
.Ss Decoding UTF-8
The lookups take codepoints; these three turn UTF-8 bytes into them without
branching.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_load_point
loads up to four bytes from
.Fa bytes
into a little-endian word:
.Li bytes[0]
lands in bits 0\(en7,
.Li bytes[1]
in bits 8\(en15, and so on.
Only bytes below
.Fa cap
are read, and the bytes past it read as 0, so it never reads at or past
.Fa bytes No + Fa cap
and a sequence that ends flush against an unmapped page is safe to load.
.Fa bytes
must not be
.Dv NULL ,
even when
.Fa cap
is 0.
It takes a pointer and a length rather than a
.Vt t_buffer
on purpose, so a scanning loop can advance the pointer without building a
fat pointer on every step.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_decode
decodes the UTF-8 sequence starting at the low byte of
.Fa w ,
as loaded by
.Fn xft_xid_load_point .
The lead byte gives the sequence length and any bytes of
.Fa w
past it are ignored.
The sequence is rejected if
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the lead byte is a continuation byte
.Pq 0x80\(en0xBF
or 0xF8\(en0xFF;
.It
a following byte is not a continuation byte, which includes one cut off by
.Fa cap ;
.It
the encoding is overlong;
.It
the result is a surrogate
.Pq U+D800\(enU+DFFF
or is past U+10FFFF.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_codepoint_len
returns the sequence length that the lead byte in the low 8 bits of
.Fa codepoint
announces.
The higher bits are ignored, so it takes a raw byte or a word from
.Fn xft_xid_load_point
alike.
It looks at the lead byte only: 0xC0, 0xC1 and 0xF5\(en0xF7 get a length
although no valid sequence starts with them, so validate with
.Fn xft_xid_decode
first.
Once a decode succeeds, the length is the number of bytes to advance.
.Ss Generating the tables
The tables are built from
.Pa DerivedCoreProperties.txt
of the Unicode Character Database:
.Pp
.Lk https://www.unicode.org/Public/UCD/latest/ucd/DerivedCoreProperties.txt
.Pp
The generator,
.Nm gentable ,
lives in
.Pa subprojects/gentable
and links
.Nm xft
itself, so the table is bootstrapped: the library is first built without
the xid module, then
.Nm gentable
regenerates
.Pa src/xid/xft_xidtable.c .
.Bl -enum
.It
Put
.Pa DerivedCoreProperties.txt
in the source root.
Configuring fails if it is missing.
.It
Configure and build with
.Dv XFT_BOOTSTRAP_XID
on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cmake -S . -B build-xid -DXFT_BOOTSTRAP_XID=ON
cmake --build build-xid
.Ed
.Pp
This builds
.Pa libxft.a
without
.Pa src/xid ,
forces
.Dv XFT_BUILD_GENTABLE
on, and runs
.Nm gentable
from the source root through the
.Ql xid-table
target, which is part of the default build.
.It
Build normally; the xid module is compiled in again.
.El
.Pp
.Nm gentable
keeps only lines whose property field is
.Ql XID_Start
or
.Ql XID_Continue ;
comments and every other property are skipped.
Both are collected into one array, each entry tagged with OR'd flags: the
low bits give its shape
.Pq Dv LITERAL No or Dv RANGE
and the high bits its property
.Pq Dv START No or Dv CONTINUE ,
so an
.Ql XID_Start
range is
.Li 0x8001 .
The emitter then writes one table per property from that array.
It writes the output through a 4 KiB buffer and opens it without
.Dv O_CREAT ,
so the
.Ql xid-table
target touches the file first.
.Pp
The source data should be refetched once a year, when a new Unicode
version is published.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_xid_is_start
and
.Fn xft_xid_is_continue
return
.Dv true
if
.Fa cp
is in the set and
.Dv false
otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_load_point
returns the first
.Fa cap
bytes of
.Fa bytes ,
at most four, zero-extended.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_decode
returns the decoded codepoint, or 0 if the sequence is invalid.
U+0000 also decodes to 0; a caller that must tell the two apart checks
whether the low byte of
.Fa w
is 0.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xid_codepoint_len
returns 1 for a lead byte in 0x00\(en0x7F, 2 for 0xC0\(en0xDF, 3 for
0xE0\(en0xEF, 4 for 0xF0\(en0xF7, and 0 for a continuation byte or
0xF8\(en0xFF.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_size	ident_len(const t_u32 *cps, t_size n)
{
	t_size	i;

	if (n == 0 || !xft_xid_is_start(cps[0]))
		return (0);
	i = 1;
	while (i < n && xft_xid_is_continue(cps[i]))
		i++;
	return (i);
}
.Ed
.Pp
The same scan over UTF-8 bytes; an invalid or truncated sequence decodes to
0, which is in neither set, so the scan stops there:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_size	ident_len_utf8(const t_u8 *s, t_size n)
{
	t_size	i;

	if (!xft_xid_is_start(xft_xid_decode(xft_xid_load_point(s, n))))
		return (0);
	i = xft_xid_codepoint_len(s[0]);
	while (i < n && xft_xid_is_continue(xft_xid_decode(
				xft_xid_load_point(s + i, n - i))))
		i += xft_xid_codepoint_len(s[i]);
	return (i);
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Pa examples/xid/utf8_idents.c
is a complete program built on the same scan: it maps a file, lists each
identifier with its byte offset and length, and reports the bytes that do
not decode.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_ctype 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh STANDARDS
Unicode Standard Annex #31, Unicode Identifiers and Syntax.
.Pp
The UTF-8 decoder follows RFC 3629 and Unicode Standard, Section 3.9.
